01:07:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question, and it highlights a common mix-up between **evaporation** and **boiling**. The short answer is: water doesn’t need to reach 100°C to evaporate. The sun provides energy that speeds up a process that happens at almost any temperature. Here’s the breakdown: ### Evaporation vs. Boiling - **Boiling** occurs at a specific temperature (100°C at sea level) when the vapor pressure of the water equals the surrounding atmospheric pressure. At this point, bubbles of vapor form throughout the liquid, and the whole volume is actively turning into gas. - **Evaporation**, on the other hand, happens only at the *surface* of the water and can occur at any temperature (even near freezing!). It’s a process where individual water molecules near the surface gain enough kinetic energy to break free from the liquid and become gas. ### How the Sun Drives Evaporation Even at room temperature, some water molecules are moving fast enough to escape. The sun’s radiation adds energy to the water, raising its temperature (though usually well below 100°C). This does two things: 1. It increases the average kinetic energy of the molecules, so *more* of them have enough speed to overcome the attractive forces of the liquid. 2. It speeds up the rate of evaporation dramatically. That’s why puddles dry up faster on a warm, sunny day than on a cold, cloudy one — not because the water boils, but because the sun boosts the surface evaporation rate. So the 100°C fact is only about boiling, not about the everyday evaporation that drives the water cycle. The sun simply doesn’t need to bring the oceans to a boil to make them evaporate — it just gives countless surface molecules the little extra kick they need to fly off into the air.
