01:08:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a completely fair point—and you’re right to feel a little misled. Your memory of what you were taught isn't wrong, but it's almost certainly a case of simplification that got stuck in your head as the whole truth. Let me break down why this happens so often. ### The Likely Truth: The Textbooks (Probably) Didn’t Say That First, a gentle correction: almost no science textbook would literally say "water evaporates at 100°C." They will say "water **boils** at 100°C" or "the **boiling point** of water is 100°C." But here’s the thing—when you’re 12 years old and learning this for the first time, your brain often smushes those two ideas together: *liquid → gas = boiling = 100°C*. The word “evaporate” gets thrown in casually, and the memory of the specific vocabulary fades. Years later, you remember "water turns to vapor at 100 degrees," and it feels like that's what you were taught verbatim. ### Why the Simplification Happens in Early Science Classes Teachers have a tough job: they need to make an invisible, probabilistic process understandable to a room full of kids. Boiling has drama. It’s got bubbles, steam, a specific number you can memorize (100°C), and a physical change you can literally watch. Evaporation is boring—puddles slowly disappearing. So early lessons often anchor on the boiling point as *the* temperature for liquid-to-gas when talking about phase changes, and then later (or not at all) they clarify that evaporation is a separate, quieter process. They do it because: - **100°C is a concrete fact**, perfect for tests. - **Evaporation is messy**: it depends on humidity, wind, surface area, and happens at any temperature. That's harder to test. - **The water cycle unit** often does cover evaporation (see a puddle dry on a cool day), but it’s taught separately from the “states of matter” unit about boiling points. The connection isn’t always made explicit. ### The Language Trap Even in everyday English, we use “evaporate” loosely to mean “disappear into vapor,” which naturally includes boiling. If you say “I boiled the water until it all evaporated,” nobody corrects you. So the verbal overlap adds to the mental melding. The truth that a good high school chemistry or physics class would hammer home is: - **Boiling**: rapid vaporization throughout the liquid at a specific temperature (100°C). - **Evaporation**: slow, surface-only vaporization at any temperature. ### So, Does Water “Evaporate” at 100°C? Technically, at the boiling point, the *evaporation rate* becomes extremely fast because the whole liquid is now producing vapor, but we usually just call the whole thing boiling. It’s not wrong to say water is evaporating *during* boiling, but it’s also not the definition of evaporation. In summary, textbooks do clarify it, but the nuance often gets lost in the mental archive, replaced by the more vivid boiling point fact. You’re now in on the secret that so many people miss: the sun is doing the former, quiet process on a massive scale, never needing to bring the oceans to a rolling boil. Your old teacher could have put it better, but hey—now you get to be the one who explains it to the next person!
