t less dramatically at lower temperatures. ### The Sun as a Gentle Simmer So, cooking doesn’t prove that evaporation starts at 100°C—it actually proves the opposite if you look closely. The sun is like the planet’s biggest simmer burner: it supplies a constant input of energy, not enough to make the oceans bubble, but more than enough to send huge volumes of invisible water vapor into the sky. The ocean’s surface is like a pot of water left on the warmest setting of a stove—it never boils, but the water level slowly drops over hours. That’s evaporation at work. The old textbook diagrams and cooking instructions aren’t lying; they’re just focused on a specific, dramatic version of a process that’s always humming along, boiling or not. You’ve been noticing the loud, visible finale (boiling) and understandably thinking that’s the whole song. Now you know the whole song—the quiet, invisible one—is playing every time a puddle shrinks or a cloud forms over the sea, no 100°C required.
