arate clear zippered bags and put them both on a warm (not hot) windowsill for a few hours. - The bag with the damp cloth will develop tiny water droplets on the inside of the plastic, while the dry bag stays clear. The droplets had to come from the damp cloth. They didn't magically pass through the plastic; they were invisible vapor released by the damp cloth, then trapped and condensed on the bag's inner surface. No boiling needed—just gentle evaporation. ### 4. Your Own Breath on a Cold Day When you exhale on a cold winter day, you see a visible cloud of "steam." But on a warm summer day, you exhale exactly the same breath and see nothing at all. Did the water in your breath stop existing? No. Your breath is always saturated with warm, invisible water vapor. It becomes visible only when it hits cold air and condenses into tiny liquid droplets—just like the steam from a kettle. The vapor itself is invisible; you only see its condensation. Your body is a constant, 37°C evaporation machine, and you're exhaling invisible vapor right now, as you read this, without a single wisp of "steam." --- These demonstrations show that water's transformation into invisible gas is not an excuse—it's a measurable, repeatable, catchable phenomenon. The reason "steam" only appears at boiling is because boiling produces such a massive surge of invisible vapor that the air can't hold it all without some of it condensing into a visible cloud. But the vapor itself, the actual gas phase of water, is always there, silently moving from puddles and towels and oceans into the air, no matter the temperature. Does that move the idea from "convenient excuse" to something you can verify with your own hands?
