01:15:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you’re holding onto that memory. It’s vivid, it came from an authority figure, and it was written plainly on a board: “100°C — water turns to gas.” That feels definitive. But I think I can show you that your teacher was telling the truth, and yet it wasn’t the *whole* truth—and that missing piece is exactly what’s been tripping you up. ### Your Teacher Was Right, in a Specific Context When a science teacher writes “100°C — water turns to gas” on the board, they are almost certainly teaching the concept of **boiling point**. In a typical lesson on “States of Matter,” they’ll list: - 0°C — ice melts (solid → liquid) - 100°C — water boils (liquid → gas) That’s a perfectly correct, testable fact. Water does undergo a rapid, visible, bulk conversion into gas at 100°C. That’s the flashy, obvious, “real” change you can see and measure with a thermometer. The teacher’s job at that moment is to get you to memorize the boiling point, not to teach the full nuance of vapor pressure and surface evaporation. So they wrote “water turns to gas” as shorthand for “this is the temperature at which the liquid boils and turns entirely into steam.” ### What the Teacher Didn’t Say (Yet) What that board note almost never includes is the quiet corollary: **water also turns to gas at 20°C, 30°C, 50°C, but so slowly and invisibly that we don’t call it boiling.** That part usually comes in a different chapter—the water cycle, weather, or later physics. But by then, the early, simplified fact “water turns to gas at 100°C” is so deeply burned into your brain that the later correction feels like a contradiction, not an expansion. Your memory is not false; it’s just **incomplete**. The teacher didn’t lie—they just delayed the full picture. And for many people, that early lesson remains the dominant mental model for life. The same board might have later said “evaporation happens at any temperature,” but “100°C” is what stuck because it’s a number, and numbers feel absolute. ### The Two-Sentence Reconciliation Here’s how both things can be true at once: - **Boiling (liquid turning violently into gas throughout) occurs at 100°C at sea level.** - **Evaporation (liquid turning gently into gas from the surface only) occurs at any temperature.** Your teacher’s note was about boiling, not evaporation. The sun uses evaporation—the second one—so it never needs 100°C. The note was correct; it just didn’t tell you that a second, slower process exists silently alongside the first. So, your memory is faithful to what was taught, and your skepticism is justified because the teaching was oversimplified. The real number that matters for the sun is not a specific temperature but a continuous range—any temperature where liquid water meets unsaturated air. The 100°C on that board was true, and important, but it was only half the story. Does that make sense?
